CORE
main.c
--unwind 3 --rounds 2 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --no-assertions --datarace
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
As `pointer_read_target`, but the dereference is `c.slots[j]->next` with `j`
constrained through a called function. The value set then does not know the
offset at which `c.slots[j]` points into its object, and
value_set_dereferencet writes each link of the chain as
`POINTER_OBJECT(p) == POINTER_OBJECT(&a) ? byte_extract(a, ...) : ...`, not
`p == &a ? a : ...`. Only the second form was recognised as a link, so both
heap objects were recorded as read, with only their offset conditions, and
mains read of slots[0]- under mutex[0] was paired with the threads
write of slots[1]->next under mutex[1] as a race.

Reported FAILED before the fix, SUCCESSFUL after. Reduced from
goblint-regression/09-regions_19-nested_nr (no-data-race, expected true),
which the name-blind wrapper reported FAILED at u16 r4.

See `pointer_read_target_object_race`: the same dereference, with the thread
writing slots[1] under the other mutex, really races and must stay FAILED.
